che Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"DFG Bi 2086/1-1"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01HG005084"},{"id":null,"name":"McLauglin Centre Accelerator","grant_id":"MC-2022-02"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01HG00583"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"T32GM008347"},{"id":null,"name":"NIH/NCI Ruth L. Kirschstein National Research F30 Award","grant_id":"5F30CA257227"}],"categories":["Genetics","Biological Network"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z6mdxvzwky","title":"Supplementary Figures S1–S4 and Tables S3–S10 for the Article 'Comprehensive Dissection of Rectal Cancer Organoids in Response to Chemoradiation'","description":"Supplementary figure S1-4 and tables S3-S10 of the article named 'Comprehensive Dissection of Rectal Cancer Organoids in Responses to Chemoradiation'","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-15T18:57:11.801Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z6mdxvzwky","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z6mdxvzwky"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaoya","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tao","last_name":"Lv","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ye","last_name":"Yao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juefeng","last_name":"Wan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lijun","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fan","last_name":"Xia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoxue","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuanchuang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guoxiang","last_name":"Fu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yun","last_name":"Deng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mengxue","last_name":"Pan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiang","last_name":"Guo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinxin","last_name":"Rao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peiyuan ","last_name":"Tang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaomeng","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yi","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liping","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yaqi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jing","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hui","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guichao","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Min","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Junjie","last_name":"Peng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanjun","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianjun","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guoqiang","last_name":"Hua","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"31470826"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"31670858"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82102833"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"81773357"}],"categories":["Radiation Therapy","Chemotherapy","Rectal Cancer","Clinical Oncology","Synergism","Antagonism","Personalized Therapy","Organoid"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j5dbtzgbkt","title":"A comprehensive human gastric cancer organoid biobank captures tumor subtype heterogeneity and enables therapeutic screening. Yan HHN et al. Mendeley Dataset 4","description":"Comparison of gene expression, DNA copy number and oncogenic protein expression by immunostaining between gastric cancer organoids against matched organoids after long-term culture, tumor tissue, and organoids derived from a 2nd tumor region, dysplasia or lymph node metastasis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-01T17:21:02.794Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j5dbtzgbkt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j5dbtzgbkt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Helen HN","last_name":"Yan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suet Yi","last_name":"Leung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stomach Cancer","Organoid"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xvk2gk4hw3","title":"Data of Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","description":"Raw data for figures in Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-25T15:27:04.321Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xvk2gk4hw3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xvk2gk4hw3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Developmental Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ptv86nczbv","title":"A flexible and low-cost spatially resolved transcriptomics strategy making molecular tissue profiling a widely available solution","description":"Spatially-resolved transcriptomics methodologies are revolutionizing our understanding of complex tissues, but their elevated costs represent still a bottle-neck for their democratization. In this work we present a low-cost strategy for manufacturing molecularly double-barcoded DNA arrays, enabling large-scale spatially-resolved transcriptomics studies.  We applied this technique to spatially resolve gene expression in several human brain organoids, including the reconstruction of a 3-dimensional view from multiple consecutive sections, revealing gene expression divergencies throughout the tissue.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-23T17:31:28.646Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ptv86nczbv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ptv86nczbv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Marco Antonio","last_name":"Mendoza-Parra","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04w6kn183","name":"Fondation pour la Recherche Médicale","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Transcriptomics","Spatial Analysis","DNA Microarrays","Organoid"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fhxws27t45","title":"A comprehensive human gastric cancer organoid biobank captures tumor subtype heterogeneity and enables therapeutic screening. Yan HHN et al. Mendeley Dataset 8","description":"Characterization of the entire cohort of gastric tumor and normal organoids by H&E staining of organoid cultures and matched primary frozen tissue, bright field microscopy, and TP53 staining in organoid cultures and matched primary frozen tissue","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-01T17:22:27.088Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fhxws27t45","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fhxws27t45"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Helen HN","last_name":"Yan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suet Yi","last_name":"Leung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stomach Cancer","Organoid"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rd2sbgm35y","title":"A comprehensive human gastric cancer organoid biobank captures tumor subtype heterogeneity and enables therapeutic screening. Yan HHN et al. Mendeley Dataset 13","description":"Differential gene expression analysis between gastric cancer organoids and frozen tumor tissues, as well as geneset enrichment analysis (GSEA) and pathway enrichment analysis, and raw data included in excel files","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-01T17:22:55.267Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rd2sbgm35y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rd2sbgm35y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Helen HN","last_name":"Yan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suet Yi","last_name":"Leung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stomach Cancer","Organoid"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hr94h42xdc","title":"Data from: Patient-derived organoids from metastatic colorectal cancer mirror tumor heterogeneity and predict patient survival and drug sensitivity","description":"Datasets Data S1 to S5 are generated in the study “Patient-derived organoids from metastatic colorectal cancer mirror tumor heterogeneity and predict patient survival and drug sensitivity”. The data originate from patient-derived organoids (PDOs) and corresponding colorectal liver metastases (CRLMs) and include genomic, transcriptomic, proteomic, and pharmacological measurements. All datasets are linked through a common sample_id, enabling cross-referencing between molecular, phenotypic, and drug response data from the same samples.\n\nData S1 – Somatic mutation profiles\nTargeted sequencing results from a custom 20-gene panel in PDOs and matched tumor tissues. Each row represents a detected variant. Columns include: sample_id, patient, sample_type, chromosome, position, ref, alt, n_ref_count, n_alt_count, t_ref_count, t_alt_count, maf (mutant allele fraction), filter, gene, func, syn, and AAChange (variant annotation including transcript, exon, nucleotide and amino acid change).\n\nData S2 – RNA sequencing gene expression counts\nGene-level RNA sequencing count matrix of PDO samples. Rows correspond to genes and columns correspond to PDO sample_id. Columns include: ensembl_gene_id, entrezgene_id, hgnc_symbol, followed by columns representing individual PDO samples containing raw read counts mapped to each gene.\n\nData S3 – Protein expression from multiplex immunohistochemistry\nQuantitative protein expression measurements obtained from multiplex immunohistochemistry (mIHC) analysis of PDO samples. Each row represents one protein measurement in a given PDO sample. Columns include: sample_id, Prot_marker, mIHC_stain_no (multiplex panel identifier), AB_order (antibody staining order), Fluor (Opal fluorophore), mean_express_PDO (mean fluorescence intensity), area_pixels (analyzed image area), and Slide (slide identifier).\n\nData S4 – Raw drug sensitivity screening measurements\nPrimary measurements from medium-throughput drug screening assays performed on PDOs. Each row corresponds to one well measurement in a drug screening plate. Columns include: sample_id, sample_id_drums, run_id, assay_no, library_id, compound_name, compound_fimm, compound_drums, compound_type, concentration, concentration_unit, drow, dcol, plate, signal (raw luminescence), and viability (normalized cell viability).\n\nData S5 – Drug sensitivity scores (DSS)\nSummary matrix of drug response profiles derived from the raw screening measurements in Data S4. Rows correspond to PDO samples and columns correspond to tested compounds and run_ids. Drug names without suffix were caclulated based on the runs indicated in the run_id_harmonize and drugs with suffix _lib1 or _lib2 indicate the drug library and run_id in which the compound was tested for the respective PDO sample. The values represent Drug Sensitivity Scores (DSS), a quantitative metric calculated from dose–response curves that integrates both drug potency and efficacy to summarize the overall sensitivity of each PDO to a given compound.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-20T18:07:19.094Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hr94h42xdc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hr94h42xdc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kushtrim","last_name":"Kryeziu","orcid":"0000-0002-6633-4818"},{"first_name":"Anita","last_name":"Sveen","orcid":"0000-0001-8219-6251"},{"first_name":"Ragnhild","last_name":"A. Lothe","orcid":"0000-0002-1693-1032"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Mutation","RNA Sequencing","Anticancer Drug","Immunohistochemistry","Colorectal Cancer","Liver Metastasis","Translational Oncology","Organoid","Cell Viability"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wgmfxds99c","title":"hESC-derived thalamic organoids form reciprocal projections when fused with cortical organoids, Xiang et al.","description":"\tHuman brain organoid techniques have rapidly advanced to facilitate investigating human brain development and diseases. Recent reports in generation and fusion of regionally defined brain organoids provide further opportunities to investigate the specific brain domains and their interactions. Focus of brain organoids has been to generate telencephalon due to its direct relevance in a variety of forebrain disorders. Despite its importance as a relay hub between cortex and peripheral tissues, the investigation of three-dimensional (3D) organoid model for the human thalamus has not yet been explored. Here, we describe a method to differentiate human embryonic stem cells (hESCs) to the thalamic organoids (hThOs) that specifically recapitulate the development of thalamus. Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) revealed a formation of distinct thalamic lineage cells, a lineage divergence from telencephalic lineage. Importantly, we developed a 3D system to create the reciprocal projections between thalamus and cortex by fusing the two distinct region-specific organoids representing the developing thalamus or cortex. Our study provides a platform for understanding human thalamic development and modeling circuit organizations and related disorders in the brain.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-22T22:38:53.481Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wgmfxds99c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wgmfxds99c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yoshiaki","last_name":"Tanaka","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yangfei","last_name":"Xiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"In-Hyun","last_name":"Park","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing","Brain","Thalamus","Organoid"],"journal_issn":["19345909"]}],"total_records":135498,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":132,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135498,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":134800,"restricted":68,"embargoed":630},"top_funders_stats":[{"i